**Short Story:**  
Blix “Sparkshank” Glimmergear, a gnome warlock with a flair for the dramatic, strutted into Stranglethorn Vale with his elegant succubus, Lilithra, by his side. “Fear not, little one,” he declared, patting her hip as she shimmered into existence. “We’ll *seduce* this jungle!” But when a pack of Jungle Stalkers closed in, Lilithra’s *Seduction* failed—*they weren’t humanoid*. Blix barely escaped, his health bleeding out. Later, cornered by a feral panther, he summoned a voidwalker just in time; its *Torment* pulled aggro, and *Consume Shadows* kept him alive between fights. Exhausted, he muttered, “She’s beautiful… but the voidwalker’s *practical*.” He dismissed Lilithra—not out of malice, but necessity—and summoned a silent, shadowy guardian instead.

**Answer:**  
In Classic WoW, warlocks *start* with the Voidwalker as their default pet, and it’s the only pet available at level 10 when they first learn to summon. Since leveling often begins before acquiring the *Summon Succubus* spell (at level 28), and many players stick with their first pet for consistency, the Voidwalker remains ubiquitous—even though the Succubus is superior for questing. Additionally, the Voidwalker’s *Sacrifice* and *Consume Shadows* provide crucial survivability in early zones, making it a reliable “tank-and-heal” companion when gear and skill are still developing. [end of text]


